s fall into natural groups: {5,7,9} (short, bottom-heavy things), {0,2,4,6} (one family sharing a hidden feature), 1 and 3 (tall narrow things), 8 (its own strongly-weighted unit).\n\n**Reasoning**\n\n- MNIST / EMNIST-digits / KMNIST are centred in a 20×20 box with a 4-pixel blank margin; the border weights would then sit at initialisation scale. Here border weights are large, especially on the top and bottom rows, which rules out digit-style centred datasets.\n- The dominant features are long vertical edges spanning most of the image height, a wide central vertical band, broad shoulder/upper-body regions, and a separate set of detectors for low horizontal masses in the bottom third. That is the signature of clothing silhouettes (trousers, dresses, coats, shirts) versus footwear (sandals, sneakers, boots) rather than of handwritten strokes.\n- The output grouping fits Fashion-MNIST's label order precisely: 5/7/9 = sandal/sneaker/ankle boot (all negative on the \"tall object\" unit, shared bottom-region units, and unit 14 splitting sneaker+boot from the airy sandal); 0/2/4/6 = T-shirt/pullover/coat/shirt (upper-body garments sharing unit 2); 1 and 3 = trouser/dress (tall, narrow); 8 = bag, with its own dedicated unit 12.\n- 10 classes, 28×28 grayscale, full-frame objects, this class structure → Fashion-MNIST.\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion-MNIST (Zalando article images, 28×28 grayscale, 10 classes).\n\n**Classes:** 0 T-shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence:** 72%",
